@@s001 Code: weighted interval scheduling in Java
@@s002 Code: weighted interval scheduling in Java
@@s003 Jobs with values: sorting by end, p(j) with binary search, and the recurrence take or skip.
@@s004 Sorting and finding p(j)
@@s005 For each job I need the number of compatible jobs that come before it. After sorting by end, the answer is found with a binary search.
@@s006 Sorting and finding p(j)
@@s007 I sort by end: this way the recurrence goes through the jobs in time order.
@@s008 Binary search over the previous jobs: I look for how many finish before the start of j.
@@s009 The job with index mid − 1 is compatible with j: I search further to the right.
@@s010 Syntax notes
@@s011 <code>long</code> for the values: the sum of the values can exceed <code>int</code>.
@@s012 The recurrence
@@s013 <code>opt[j]</code> is the maximum between taking job j (its value plus the best of the compatible ones) and skipping it.
@@s014 The recurrence
@@s015 I take job j: its value plus <code>opt</code> of the compatible jobs, that is <code>opt[p(j)]</code>.
@@s016 The maximum between taking it and skipping it.
@@s017 Syntax notes
@@s018 <code>job[j - 1][2]</code>: the value is in the third column. The indices of <code>opt</code> start from 1 because <code>opt[0] = 0</code> is the base case.
@@s019 Running it
@@s020 Example of input and output (actually compiled and run).
